iOS port of GNU Emacs
=====================

This directory contains the iOS-specific scaffolding for building
Emacs as an iOS application bundle distributable through the App
Store.  The port is being landed incrementally; see the
"Suggested implementation order" in the design document for the
sequence of follow-up commits.

The C-side terminal driver, font and pasteboard bridges live under
src/ios*.{m,h,c} and src/iosvfs.c.  This directory holds only:

  Emacs.base/                Bundle template (Info.plist, entitlements,
                             launch screen, icons), assembled into the
                             output .app at build time.
  Emacs/                     Objective-C app shell (main.m,
                             AppDelegate, SceneDelegate).  These are
                             linked into the same Mach-O as the Emacs
                             core, and call into ios_main() from
                             src/ios.m once UIKit has finished
                             launching.
  Makefile.in                Bundle-assembly orchestration; mirrors
                             nextstep/Makefile.in's tar + copy +
                             codesign approach.

Building
--------

Building the iOS port requires:

  * A macOS host with Xcode installed.
  * The iOS SDK (use `xcrun --sdk iphoneos --show-sdk-path').
  * For on-device installation, an Apple Developer account and a
    valid provisioning profile.

The current scaffolding is not yet complete:

  * The recursive XCONFIGURE=ios cross-configure path (analogous to
    the XCONFIGURE=android path at configure.ac:29) is not yet
    wired up.  Until it lands, ./configure --with-ios is a
    documented placeholder and `make -C ios all' will report a
    clear error explaining that the cross-compiled emacs binary is
    missing.
  * cross/xcode-build/ -- the Xcode/clang cross-compile rules that
    produce the libemacs.a + emacs binary for iOS -- has not yet
    been added.

When both are in place the eventual build sequence will be:

  ./autogen.sh
  ./configure --with-ios --with-ios-deployment-target=15.0
  make ios-bundle           # or: make && make -C ios all

producing ios/Emacs.app, which can be installed in the Simulator with

  xcrun simctl install booted ios/Emacs.app
  xcrun simctl launch booted org.gnu.emacs

On first launch Emacs writes its pdumper image into

  <sandbox>/Library/Application Support/Emacs/emacs.pdmp

(roughly 10-30 seconds depending on hardware), and subsequent
launches mmap it sub-second.

App Store compliance notes
--------------------------

* No JIT (no native compilation, no dynamic module loading).
* No fork/exec; Emacs subprocess primitives error out.
* Sandboxed filesystem; user files live in the per-app Documents
  directory and Files-app picked URLs.
* pdumper image is data, not executable code, and is generated on
  first launch into the writable Application Support directory.
